-- allium: 3
-- rtdb-v1.allium specifies the consumer-facing ranked read over this search path.
-- fts.allium
--
-- Full-text search over a table's documents, answered at any valid time and system time.
--
-- The unit indexed is the text run: every version of one entity in a trie data file whose indexed values are the same, with the rectangles of valid time × system time where one of them is visible, as the scan's own resolver decides them over the file.
-- A trie may carry one search file per index definition over its own data file's runs.
-- The members of a partition group (trie-cat.allium: PartGroup) may share one search file over their runs, deleted with the last member.
-- A search pack may also cover a set of settled tries with disjoint IID prefixes and one recency.
-- A search file only nominates candidates.
-- Which version of an entity is visible at the query's valid and system time is decided by the scan's bitemporal resolution, as it is for every other predicate, and MATCH is re-checked on the resolved row.
-- So a search file that is missing costs a query time and never changes its result.
--
-- An owned clear-text declaration comes from the authenticated principal's protected search-path catalog.
-- The artefact lane feeds its one explicitly declared text column to the same search-file builder.
-- An owned declaration can name an ordinary UTF-8 column or an optional fact struct with a single required, nullable UTF-8 value field.
-- The fact wrapper distinguishes a missing fact field from a present null, and both contribute no search text.
-- The owned path keeps its declared column and carrier when the relation declaration is widened.
-- An owned search path declares its analyzer, stored in its catalog row when it is not `simple`; its files, held searches and staged overlays tokenise with it, and one held search ranks paths of one analyzer.
-- Root and world replay keep the original carrier, including the distinction between an omitted field and its present null value.
-- Published files include the ordered definition version, artifact identity, and exact token length of each run.
-- Owned ranking uses published files covering the held cut or a bounded projection of its exact visible identities and declared text.
-- Both ranking paths apply the same typed overlay shadows before computing corpus statistics.
-- Shadow text comes from the selected Arrow field when its presence marker names that field.
-- Held corpus accounting uses the complete identity's typed payload size and the fixed allowance for its retained entry.
-- A declared optional text column with only null or absent values contributes an empty corpus.
-- Owned runs carry their full typed identity.
-- Owned ranked candidates share a projected primary cursor in bounded batches.
-- Each cursor retains the candidate's complete keys as slices of their posting file's Arrow buffers.
-- The scan checks each candidate's complete Arrow identity, microsecond revision, and declared text leg at the held cut.
-- The ranker uses posting frequencies and stored lengths when the winning revision identifies an unambiguous event in its run.
-- Overlapping events at the same revision use the resolved text to verify the match and score.
-- Owned ranking uses only the held corpus and breaks score ties by the complete typed identity, then by the requested partition ordinal.
-- Root and hosted World Flight replies retain the verified rank columns through encoding and check the held relations again before release.
-- Retained-world ranking checks for a placed world host before decoding the Flight request.
-- Row count, token count, and term frequencies use only runs whose temporal visibility is exact at that cut.
-- Public SQL search definitions remain scoped to the public catalog.
--
-- The core knows a search file only as an artefact of kind `search`: trie-cat.allium records artefacts on trie entries, db.allium carries ArtefactsAdded through the leader, and gc.allium deletes artefacts with their owner.
-- This spec owns what an artefact of kind `search` holds, which definitions and jobs produce them, and how a query reads them.
--
-- See: rust/crates/search, rust/crates/text, rust/crates/maintenance/src/artefact.rs, rust/crates/query/src/accelerate.rs; plan: fts-implementation.adoc

use "./trie-cat.allium" as trie_cat
use "./db.allium" as db

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Given
------------------------------------------------------------

given {
    database: db/Database
    trie_catalog: trie_cat/TrieCatalog
    search_definitions: SearchDefinitions
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Values
------------------------------------------------------------

value SearchIndexDefinition {
    -- A document of `xt.search_indexes`, keyed by the index's name.
    name: String
    table: trie_cat/TableRef
    -- Only the UTF-8 leg of each named column is indexed; other legs of the same column contribute no terms.
    columns: List<String>
    -- The analyzer that tokenises the indexed columns: `WITH (analyzer = '...')`, `simple` when the document names none (Analyzer).
    analyzer: String
    -- The defining transaction's id, so redefining an index under its name is a new version.
    version: Integer
    -- False when the index covers the current side alone: `WITH (history = false)`; a document without it covers history.
    history: Boolean

    -- Derived
    -- The artefact definition id distinguishes analyzers, analyzer versions and search file formats so their files can coexist.
    -- Redefining an index with another analyzer, or a build with another analyzer version, makes new ids, so the earlier files retire (RetireSearchFiles).
    artefact_id: "{name}~{analyzer}.a{analyzer_version}p{event_layout_version}f{format_version}"
}

value Analyzer {
    -- How an index and the queries against it turn text into tokens; one version (rtdb_text::ANALYZER_VERSION) covers every analyzer, its pinned Unicode tables, stop word lists and stemmers.
    -- `simple`: runs of alphanumeric characters, lowercased.
    -- `standard`: Unicode words (UAX #29), NFKC-normalized, lowercased, with sharp s folded to ss and final sigma to sigma.
    -- `dutch`, `english`, `french`, `german`, `italian`, `portuguese`, `spanish`: standard tokens without French or Italian elided articles, then Snowball-stemmed.
    -- Each language analyzer has a `_stop` form that also drops the language's stop words before stemming.
    name: String
}

value SearchDoc {
    -- One document per text run of a column: an entity's versions in the data file whose value in that column has the same tokens, a value that is not text having none.
    -- A version visible nowhere within the file, a delete, an erase, and a version whose column is null or absent belong to no run of the column.
    -- The builder refuses assertion-redacted source events until it can apply exact-origin scrub coverage.
    doc_id: Integer
    iid: Bytes
    member: Integer
    -- Where one of the run's versions is visible, as rtdb_temporal::visibility resolves the file's events of the entity with no bounds.
    rectangles: List<Rectangle>
}

value Rectangle {
    valid_from: Instant
    valid_to: Instant
    system_from: Instant
    system_to: Instant
}

value SearchRowVersion {
    -- The temporal order of an event within a captured table.
    iid: Bytes
    system_from: Instant
    original_transaction_position: Integer
    original_event_ordinal: Integer
}

value AcceptedEventOrigin {
    -- All fragments and relocations of one accepted event carry the same origin.
    accepted_commit: Bytes
    ordinal: Integer
    original_block: Integer
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

entity SearchDefinitions {
    -- The definitions in force: `xt.search_indexes` read at a committed basis, as of the basis's own system time in valid time too.
    -- They are documents the indexer writes, so every node reading at the same basis holds the same set, and a node reads them once per basis.
    -- A snapshot carrying a transaction's own writes reads them afresh, as of the reader's current time.
    definitions: List<SearchIndexDefinition>
}

entity SearchJob {
    -- An artefact job of kind `search`; ephemeral, like compaction.allium's CompactionJob.
    table: trie_cat/TableRef
    owner: trie_cat/TrieKey | trie_cat/PartGroup | trie_cat/ArtefactPack
    definition: SearchIndexDefinition
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- See: server config `search: {enabled, threads, writeFormat}` and NodeOptions.
    -- The configured format determines which files new jobs write.
    -- Format 16 gives each public history-enabled definition a manifest of logical posting runs.
    -- Durable logical runs build where the writer can register a staging owner with a recoverable drain witness.
    -- Hosted compactor attachments build primary compaction outputs and leave logical posting builds to the directory holder.
    -- Queries cover missing logical runs through the existing search gap paths.
    write_format: Integer = 13
    -- Off stops the lane and the accelerator together; MATCH is then answered by scanning.
    enabled: Boolean = true
    threads: Integer = 1
    -- Above this many candidates, the scan runs unnarrowed.
    max_candidates: Integer = 100000
    -- Above this many bytes of selected tries the lane should have indexed but has not, the node builds none of their search files itself.
    -- Level 0 and the partial level-1 current trie, which the lane never indexes, do not count.
    max_unindexed_bytes: Integer = 67108864
    -- Group members' data files must fit within this limit and a quarter of the node's memory budget.
    group_bytes: Integer = 536870912
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Definitions
------------------------------------------------------------

rule DefineSearchIndex {
    -- CREATE SEARCH INDEX name ON table (column, ...) [WITH (history = true | false, analyzer = 'name')] is a transaction; the indexer writes its document at the transaction's system time, valid from then on.
    -- An unknown analyzer name is refused.
    -- An index covers history by default; without it, the lane builds no historical trie's search file, and a past query reads those tries through files its node builds.
    when: SearchIndexDefined(definition)

    ensures:
        search_definitions.definitions.add(definition)
}

rule DropSearchIndex {
    -- DROP SEARCH INDEX deletes the document; the lane then retires the dropped definition's search files (RetireSearchFiles).
    when: SearchIndexDropped(name)

    ensures:
        search_definitions.definitions.remove(name)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Index lane
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Search jobs are the artefact lane's jobs (maintenance/src/artefact.rs: available_artefact_jobs) for the definitions in force:
-- a live trie above level 0, other than a level-1 current trie below the file size target, and other than a historical trie for a definition without history, lacking an artefact for the definition's artefact id and version.
-- Level 0 and the partial level-1 current trie are rewritten within a block or two, so a node builds their search files itself when a query needs them.
-- A partition group whose members are all live, lack the file, and fit the size limit is one job.
-- Otherwise each eligible member that lacks the file gets its own job.
-- Jobs run current side first, then higher levels first.

rule SearchJobRuns {
    -- Each tiered compaction job writes one bucket, so compaction leaves a potential shared search file to the lane.
    -- For other eligible outputs, compaction still builds a search file from the local data file and records it on TrieDetails before publishing.
    -- The lane builds files compaction leaves out, files for new definitions or versions, and repairs.
    -- The lane downloads the owner's data files, in bucket order for a group.
    -- Both paths read one page at a time under that page's memory reservation.
    -- The projected decoder selects event headers, the document ID and the indexed children of the put struct.
    -- The analyzer writes token bytes and offsets directly into Arrow string builders and offers a term visitor for consumers of ordered tokens.
    -- An on-demand build decodes page buffers from its retained data-file cache lease and serves the typed search pages it builds.
    -- Each build reserves the indexing heap up front in proportion to its input size.
    -- A durable build writes the segment on disk and streams it to the object store.
    -- An on-demand build retains its checked Arrow batches for the query.
    -- A job the memory budget cannot admit fails, and is eligible again at the next block.
    -- A format 15 writer seals search files with primary level-0 data at the block boundary when admitted.
    -- The format 15 lane fills missing coverage and can replace committed singleton files with one pack independently of primary compaction.
    -- A format 16 writer seals forward witnesses for the indexed fields with the primary block.
    -- The seal can publish a posting run for its L0 extent, and the independent lane fills gaps after block adoption.
    -- Queries account for that extent from primary history until the run is adopted.
    -- The posting revision and the forward witness at sealing derive from the same analyzer tokens.
    -- An independent forward build computes the revision as the analyzer visits each term.
    -- The table directory covers a sealed prefix of accepted events and carries each erased entity's latest erase frontier.
    -- The table also owns event header pages for individual primary tries.
    -- A point read takes a primary trie without entity history from the data pages its metadata routes the requested keys to, so older data narrows the read rather than sending it to a scan.
    -- Gap nomination reads each uncovered block with a live L0 trie from that trie's pages, including within the directory's sealed prefix.
    -- Gap nomination skips the directory when every block in its sealed prefix has posting coverage or a live L0 trie.
    -- An uncovered compacted block in the sealed prefix is nominated through the captured entity directory.
    -- Compacted history beyond the sealed prefix is nominated from live primary tries.
    -- Primary gap reads count the rows of each decoded page against the inspection budget.
    -- Directory gap reads count only rows from uncovered blocks that lack a live L0 trie against that budget.
    -- The per-trie point path checks recorded entity keys in history files, primary pages, and live segments.
    -- Histories with different entity keys under one IID resolve through the scan.
    -- A forward witness identifies an event and a digest of the ordered analyzer token sequence for one field.
    -- Readers authenticate the selected forward-witness range and decode Arrow from its retained bytes.
    -- A resolved event must identify itself before a forward witness can certify its score.
    -- The writer stores a search-pending intent with its staging owner before uploading the immutable body.
    -- A successful upload supplies the immutable publication for ordered adoption when the provider exposes it.
    -- An unsuccessful upload leaves the intent for collection under the same writer-drain rules.
    -- A store that requires immutable provider evidence admits a new run only with a physical version or an enrolled terminal publication.
    -- Native local Classic admits runs under the terminal-name contract of its recovery-locked store.
    -- A captured catalog routes each required term using the authenticated locator of each eligible run.
    -- A captured catalog can hold one complete term directory for each format 16 definition and analyzer generation.
    -- Each row binds a field, analyzed term and run digest to its document frequency, maximum term frequency, minimum stored length, maximum unit term score and window range.
    -- Its immutable pages authenticate rows and carry the maximum unit term score of each subtree.
    -- Each term-directory build gives newly written pages identities unique to the attempt.
    -- A run addition or replacement can publish updated rows in the same ordered change as its run receipt, under a compare-and-set on the expected directory root and active run identities.
    -- The accepted term directory and its active run set are checkpointed together.
    -- The lane can rebuild a directory from the authenticated term summaries of the active runs.
    -- A run nominates an entity and text revision for a field, with terms and positions established by that definition's analyzer.
    -- A primary rewrite replaces the corresponding history locations while preserving the text revision's posting coverage.
    -- Replacement posting runs cover the exact union of the active receipts they supersede.
    -- A merge with both replay-backed and L0-backed inputs verifies the full union at a pinned boundary and publishes a source receipt that extends the replay-backed input's receipt.
    -- The lane remembers failed merge proofs by table, definition identifier, definition version and both run digests.
    -- Each logical maintenance pass reconciles the bounded memo with the active catalog and drops pairs with a retired input.
    -- At capacity, a new failed proof displaces the last pair that the lane recorded or skipped.
    -- A remembered pair skips replay while its entry is retained, and eviction makes the pair eligible for replay again.
    -- The lane removes postings whose contributing events all precede an erase and retains postings with surviving later events.
    -- A scan uses logical negative evidence within the source extents covered by admitted runs at its pinned basis.
    -- A missing or corrupt run leaves its extent eligible for exact nomination from primary history.
    -- A scan whose bounded gap read cannot establish a complete candidate union executes the ordinary scan.
    -- A replay-backed repair verifies the boundary, source row count, exact block extents and erase frontier before publication.
    -- Ranked search and explicit entity point reads can resolve the same complete temporal history.
    when: job: SearchJob.created

    ensures:
        BuildAndUploadSearchFile(job)
        db/ArtefactsAdded(table: job.table, entries: search_entries(job)) appended to database.source_log
}

rule RetireSearchFiles {
    -- A search file is retired once its logical definition or analyzer version leaves force.
    -- A reader retains files in every supported format for the same definition version.
    -- A retired definition is one neither in force nor made after the snapshot's basis, so a definition newer than the snapshot is never retired.
    -- The lane appends ArtefactsRemoved for the retired records on the table's live tries, and once it is applied deletes their objects.
    -- It deletes as well every unrecorded object of kind `search` under a live trie for a retired definition: one a builder uploaded and failed to record.
    -- While a definition is in force, such an object needs no sweep: its job stays available, and the next builder writes the same bytes under the same key and records them.
    -- The lane looks at every table when it starts, at a table whenever its definitions in force change, and again at a table whose last look, or one of whose jobs, failed; it looks only while no job of its own is running.
    -- GC collects an artefact under a retired trie or under a group with no remaining members.
    -- A format 15 file and every superseded singleton stay available to pinned readers.
    -- Sealed level-0 search files retire once their trie is obsolete in the checkpoint and all reader holds have expired.
    -- A format 16 run retires on ordered replacement or when its definition leaves force.
    -- Collection orders abandonment of a completed upload once the source cut is installed and the staging owner has drained.
    -- An ordered upload receipt for a rejected replacement is itself eligible for retirement.
    -- Collection waits for active catalog captures, the latest recovery checkpoint, and the reader lease before releasing a retired run.
    -- Every node's lane retires on its own, so a removal may be appended more than once, and a second changes nothing.
    -- A builder on another node may still record an object a retiring lane deleted; its own lane retires that record at its next look.
    when: DefinitionsInForceChanged(table)

    ensures:
        db/ArtefactsRemoved(table: table, entries: retired_records(table)) appended to database.source_log
        RetiredSearchFilesDeleted(table)
}

rule RepairUnreadableSearchFile {
    -- A scan that finds a recorded search file missing, or its bytes unreadable as a search file, evaluates that trie from its data file and reports the file to its database's lane, which wakes for it.
    -- A file that could not be fetched at all is not reported: the store may be briefly unavailable, and the file sound.
    -- The lane drops its cached copies and reads the object afresh. If the object is missing it builds it; if its bytes are still unreadable it deletes it and builds it again; if it cannot be fetched the job fails and is retried.
    -- Rebuilding the original file reproduces its bytes and leaves its records in place.
    -- If the group build finds an original member's data file missing and the catalog has superseded or collected that member, the lane builds a file for each remaining live member that still needs one.
    -- A single ArtefactsAdded message replaces the shared records after the new files have been uploaded.
    -- A report is dropped when no live member still records that search file or the definition has left force.
    -- A damaged format 16 run with readable source inputs is repaired by ordered publication of a new immutable run over the same extents.
    -- A native repair can read the retained accepted journal up to the pinned checkpoint boundary.
    -- A native source receipt takes its lineage from the replica journal and retains that identity when the journal moves.
    -- A writable logical-search open assigns a new journal its own lineage and adopts an existing legacy lineage only for a journal with record frames.
    -- A writable open discards a valid legacy sidecar beside an empty unbound journal before any fresh lineage is published.
    -- A legacy lineage adopted into the header survives removal of the sidecar.
    -- A conflicting legacy sidecar blocks the open.
    -- A read-only legacy journal provides replay and becomes eligible for native repair after reopening with an embedded lineage.
    -- A hosted repair reads a directory root anchored in the published checkpoint and verifies the committed replica suffix through the pinned boundary.
    -- A hosted checkpoint published with a semantic serving inventory can be opened after loss of its logical search runs and term-directory pages.
    -- The installed table directory authenticates the surviving event headers and erase frontier at that boundary.
    -- Repair resolves surviving put payloads from primary data by stable event origin and checks each payload against the directory event.
    -- A source receipt binds the lineage, replica interval, table, shard, block extents, row count and erase frontier used to build the replacement.
    -- Replacement compares its extents with the expected active runs and verifies compatible source receipts before adopting the new run.
    -- A rejected replacement whose input remains active leaves that run suspect and records a serving gap while the lane continues maintaining other definitions.
    when: SearchFileUnreadable(table, owner, definition)

    ensures:
        SearchJob.created(table: table, owner: owner, definition: definition)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Query
------------------------------------------------------------

-- MATCH(column, query[, analyzer]) holds when the column's UTF-8 value holds every term of the query, both tokenised by the named analyzer (`simple` when none is named), and every phrase of it (terms between double quotes) as consecutive tokens.
-- The analyzer is a string literal, so MATCH and SEARCH_SCORE stay functions of their arguments; every search function takes it as an optional last argument.
-- RTDB_SEARCH(table, column, query[, analyzer]) ranks with the same analyzer.
-- RTDB_HYBRID(table, text_column, query, vector_column, vector, window, limit[, analyzer]) fuses, at the query's one snapshot, the first `window` rows of the exact text ranking and of the exact cosine nearest ranking by reciprocal rank fusion.
-- A row at rank r of a window, counted from 1 with ties ordered by `_iid`, contributes 1 / (60 + r); `_score` adds the text contribution, then the nearest one, and the first `limit` rows by descending `_score`, then `_iid`, are returned.
-- rtdb.owned.HybridHeld.v1 fuses a held text window and a held nearest window of the same relations at one held cut the same way, ties ordered by typed identity, then partition.
-- The analyzer splits a word longer than the index's longest token (rtdb_text::MAX_TOKEN_BYTES) into tokens of at most that length, so the index holds every token the analyzer makes.
-- SEARCH_SCORE(column, query) sums BM25 term-frequency contributions with unit weights and unit length normalization.
-- The five-argument form computes BM25 from its document count, total stored length and document frequencies.
-- The frequency vector follows the distinct term order returned by SEARCH_TERMS.
-- SEARCH_LENGTH gives the stored length of a UTF-8 document for aggregation over the admitted rows and null for any other input.
-- Each columnar expression batch prepares its constant query and fixed corpus weights once and infers each source field's text type once.
-- Evaluation streams analyzer tokens into the length, term frequencies, and phrase matches it needs.
-- SEARCH_CORPUS_FINGERPRINT hashes the ordered text id and body pairs passed as two arrays.
-- Its Arrow inputs supply each pair from the selected UTF-8 positions.
-- Each id and body is prefixed with its byte length so the input has one spelling.
-- Ranked pruning applies to the two-argument form.
--
-- A definition covers a MATCH when it indexes the MATCH's column under the MATCH's analyzer; a column indexed only under another analyzer is scanned.
-- Live postings, the commit's search delta and serving postings are kept per table, column and analyzer.
-- A ranked scan uses a definition only when its SEARCH_SCORE names the analyzer of its MATCH.
--
-- A scan with a MATCH conjunct on a column that a definition in force covers is narrowed before it opens:
--
--   1. The scan selects tries exactly as it does without MATCH.
--      A trie whose recency is at or before the earlier of the query's valid and system lower bounds holds no event the query can see, so no search file of it is read or built.
--      A recorded trie none of whose lifespan sections, read from its record's stats, meets the bounds is left unopened, and within a file a document whose section misses them is passed over.
--   2. A selected trie whose entry records a search file for that definition contributes the IIDs of its docs whose terms satisfy the query and one of whose rectangles meets the query's bounds.
--      The scan reads a shared file once and uses the runs of selected members alone for candidates and ranking.
--      When a trie records several formats for the same definition version, ordinary and held reads prefer the newest Arrow format and can still open a format 11 record on its own.
--      A ranked phrase scan can use an existing format 13 sidecar when all selected format 14 sources also have that sidecar.
--      Exact reads of formats 14 and 15 use authenticated pages for the queried terms and retain the event pages needed for row answers and temporal cuts.
--      Public reads need document pages only for the relevant scoring windows, and an exact held corpus visits every covered document page because nonmatching runs contribute to its statistics.
--   3. A selected trie with no such record, or whose file cannot be read, is read through a search file the node builds from its data file and keeps, under the same rule.
--      A data file never changes, so a kept one serves every later query of the trie.
--      Queries that need the same file at once share one build; one that fails or is cancelled leaves the next to build it.
--   4. Every live segment contributes the IIDs of its matching rows under the same rule, with recency ∞.
--      A node publishes immutable postings for a prefix of the live batches under their table, column and snapshot lineage.
--      A query uses whole pages within the prefix in its own snapshot and reads the remaining rows directly.
--      A document is cut by the events of its entity in the partition's other selected tries and live segments, as CutByLaterEvents says, and contributes only if a rectangle it keeps meets the bounds.
--   5. The scan runs restricted to the union, resolving each candidate's full history in every selected trie and live segment.
--   6. MATCH is evaluated on each resolved row, as MatchEvaluatedByTheScan says.
--
-- Past max_candidates candidates, the scan runs unnarrowed instead.
-- Past max_unindexed_bytes of tries the lane should have indexed but has not, the node builds none of them: the scan is neither narrowed nor ranked, and only rows of tries with a recorded file take MATCH from it.
--
-- When optional search construction cannot fit the query budget or the source page order is unsupported by the search format, the scan evaluates those rows from their data files.
-- The two-argument score uses the same term contributions and binary64 addition order in each partition and in SQL evaluation.
--
-- A ranked point scan with an explicit IID scope of at most 128 resolves that scope through table history and scores the resolved rows directly.
-- Other logical scans intersect the authenticated routes of every required term, starting with the term with the smallest recorded document frequency.
-- A sparse logical conjunction expands the rarest required term's posting directory first and intersects occurrences before reading phrase positions.
-- Dense posting runs expand score-bounded windows in priority order.
-- A missing or unreadable term directory schedules repair and uses each run's authenticated locator for term membership.
--
-- A point scan whose rows feed only projections and then `ORDER BY SEARCH_SCORE(column, query) DESC ... LIMIT n`, with a MATCH on the same column, can rank at one query frontier.
--
--   1. The query accounts for every partition in its pinned view before choosing a cutoff.
--      Unopened sources and live suffixes without a bound, and exact work for uncovered history, have unbounded rank.
--      Posting windows use a conservative sum of per-term bounds in scoring order.
--      Ascending integer and UUID identities can strengthen an equal-score bound when the source proves the corresponding SQL identity order.
--      Admitted live postings supply per-event term frequencies, document lengths and integer tie keys.
--      Live events beyond the admitted frontier use the analyzer over their primary Arrow text.
--      Where an earlier selection supplies an IID scope, file and live events outside that scope are discarded before scoring.
--   2. Workers expand work and nominate candidates under its bound.
--      A claimed bound covers the work through publication of its children and verified outcomes.
--      Verification resolves the candidate's full history and gathers the visible row's text, SQL rank keys and predicate columns.
--      The verifier checks the SQL predicates and scores that payload.
--      Verification reads an integer tie key from its selected Arrow cell with the scan's signed execution semantics.
--      A stale posting supplies no verified score for its revision.
--   3. The query stops when all work is exhausted, or when its verified cutoff strictly precedes every queued and claimed bound.
--      Pending verification has a bound until the verifier reports.
--      The cutoff counts the first OFFSET plus LIMIT rows across partitions.
--      A score-only bound requires a strictly lower score than the cutoff.
--      The signed zeroes have equal score order.
--      An invalid bound keeps the affected work eligible.
--   4. The scan gathers projected columns for retained versions after deciding the query's prefix.
--      A located primary page supplies event headers, the projected fields and the fields needed for residual MATCH.
--      If its final gather exceeds available memory, the scan can stream from the narrowed IID selection.
--      The SQL sort applies the final ordering and window.
--
-- A candidate-budget miss without an IID scope skips unranked narrowing if the ranked candidate union already contains more distinct IIDs than its limit.
-- This union is a lower bound for the unranked candidate union and counts each IID once across its pending and handed-out events.
-- Other declines use unranked narrowing, including an exhausted verification budget or a candidate-budget miss where there is no distinct-IID proof.

rule CutByLaterEvents {
    -- A document's rectangles come from its own file's events; an event of the same entity elsewhere in the partition, in a selected trie or live segment, cuts them.
    -- A put or delete whose system-from is later than a rectangle's takes the event's valid interval from that system time on.
    -- A later erase removes the whole rectangle, including a rectangle whose system interval ended before the erase.
    -- An earlier event takes nothing.
    -- An event at the same instant takes nothing: the scan orders such events itself, so the document stays a candidate and is no longer exact.
    -- The tries cut by are those step 1 keeps, each found for the entity by its IID path and its file's key table, and the live segments by their kept keys; a trie step 1 leaves out holds no cut the query can see.
    -- A document is exactly visible where a rectangle it keeps meets the bounds, no tie touched it, and no trie left unopened by its sections covers its entity: such a trie may hold a cut, a delete say.
    when: ScanWithMatch(scan, predicate)

    ensures:
        EachCandidateCutByItsEntitysLaterEvents(scan)
}

rule MatchIsAResidualPredicate {
    when: ScanWithMatch(scan, predicate)

    let candidates = search_candidates(scan, predicate)

    ensures:
        ScanRestrictedToIids(scan, candidates)
        FilterResolvedRows(scan, predicate)
}

rule MatchEvaluatedByTheScan {
    -- A MATCH with a constant query in a predicate the scan filters by, under NOT and IS NULL as much as bare, is a boolean column the scan evaluates on each resolved row.
    -- The scan shares its parsed constant queries and scorers between nomination, verification and residual evaluation.
    -- Narrowing retains its sorted Arrow IID scope for subsequent selection and scan resolution.
    -- The uncovered live suffix tests analyzer tokens as they are produced from each batch's selected text child.
    -- The text column is then read only for the rows the scan cannot answer otherwise, and projected only if something else needs it.
    -- A row of a trie takes MATCH from its search file for a definition covering the column: true where the document matches, null where the column is null or absent, and false otherwise.
    -- A row in a trie with no readable recorded file can take its answer from the file the node builds.
    -- A live row in the captured serving prefix can take its MATCH and score from the admitted postings for its physical Arrow position.
    -- The scan evaluates the resolved value when its position has no admitted search answer.
    when: ScanWithMatch(scan, predicate)

    ensures:
        EachResolvedRowMatchedFromItsSearchFileOrValue(scan, predicate)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

contract SearchFileLayout {
    -- What a search file holds.
    -- See: search/src/file.rs

    @invariant PureFunctionOfFileAndDefinition
        -- A search file's bytes are a deterministic function of the owner's data files, its definition and the job's identity.
        -- Every node that runs the same job writes the same object.

    @invariant BoundedArrowDirectory
        -- The format 14 artefact record locates a bounded Arrow root by byte range and SHA-256 digest.
        -- The root describes independently addressable term, posting, document and IID visibility trees.
        -- A term directory records short rank summaries inline and refers to rank pages for larger postings.
        -- Each rank branch bounds the scores of its descendant windows and may prove the first identity at its exact maximum.
        -- Each directory edge authenticates its child byte range.
        -- A negative discovery synopsis excludes term nominations; IID visibility can still read the same file.
        -- Per-storage cache entries hold authenticated immutable pages and share concurrent reads.
        -- Readers with prepaid cache allocations hold search range leases within those allocations.
        -- Coalesced remote reads retain only the selected ranges in the decoded page cache.
        -- A sparse query can read visibility for an IID in a source that did not nominate it.

    @invariant PackedDirectoriesAndVersions
        -- Format 15 authenticates term, posting, rank and IID visibility data against the root named by the held catalog.
        -- A bounded root may travel in that catalog receipt, with terminal range references that eliminate intermediate directory reads.
        -- Table routing is computed from one held catalog and uses negative term or time evidence only to omit nominations.
        -- Every primary event has its logical version, valid interval, operation, member and primary Arrow position in the visibility directory.
        -- A physical rewrite may change the member and Arrow position of an event while preserving its logical version.

    @invariant OneDocPerRun
        -- A search file has one document per text run of each column in each member's data file, so a version changing one column adds no posting to another.
        -- It records each row's run in each column, or that the row is visible with the column null or absent, or that it is visible nowhere within the file.
        -- A document's rectangles may merge when they have the same system-time interval and adjacent valid-time intervals.
        -- Each rectangle retains the originating event's system-from for cuts by events outside its file.

    @invariant RectanglesFromTheResolver
        -- A document's rectangles are where the scan's resolver, run over the file's events of the entity with no bounds, makes one of the run's versions visible.
        -- A scan at any bounds sees exactly the events whose rectangles meet them (temporal/tests/visibility.rs), so within the file a document is visible exactly where a rectangle is.

    @invariant KeysAreRecorded
        -- The file records every row's IID, system from, valid interval and operation, in data-file order within each member.
        -- A partition group's members have disjoint IID ranges and appear in bucket order, so a shared file's keys are in IID order.

    @invariant LifespanSections
        -- A document's lifespan runs from its birth, the earliest of its rectangles' later lower bound, to its death, the latest of their earlier upper bound, clipped to its trie's recency: no row of a historical trie is visible from its recency on, whatever its own file's events say.
        -- Documents fall into classes by lifespan: under one, four, sixteen, sixty-four and two hundred and fifty-six weeks, and longer.
        -- Each class's section bounds its documents' rectangles, births and deaths, and the file records it in its sidecar and in its artefact record's stats.
        -- A shared file's lifespan sections bound its full partition group, whose members share one recency.
        -- A document is visible at a point only where its birth is before the point's later coordinate and its death after the earlier, so a section whose bounds miss a query's holds nothing visible to it.
        -- A query at a past valid time but the present system time prunes little this way: only the bounds on each axis apply.

    @invariant WindowBoundsAreUpperBounds
        -- Documents fall in windows of 128 consecutive ids.
        -- For each term of a column and each window holding it, the file records the term's highest frequency there and the shortest stored length among the documents holding it, stored for a term in at least 1024 documents and read from its postings otherwise.
        -- A term's part of BM25 rises with its frequency and, its inverse document frequency being positive, falls with the length, so no document in the window scores above the sum of the parts at those, whatever the corpus.

    @invariant TermDirectoryBoundsCoverActiveRuns
        -- A usable term directory covers exactly the active runs in its generation at the held cut, including runs with no searchable terms.
        -- An unopened run's bound adds its term bounds in the scoring query's order, with zero for each scoring term absent from the run.
        -- An unopened run starts with an unknown tie position.
        -- A scan may omit a run only when authenticated required-term evidence excludes it or its bound is below the verified cutoff.
        -- A run change without a directory update invalidates the previous directory for that generation.

    @invariant RowsAndNullsAreRecorded
        -- The file records the first page and document of each member and the first row of each data page.
        -- A visible row's member, page and position within it determine MATCH in each column, null included.

    @invariant TokensAreTheResidualsTokens
        -- A search file's terms are the analyzer's tokens of each row's UTF-8 value, the same tokens MATCH evaluates on a resolved row, with their positions, so a phrase matches in the file exactly when it matches the row.
}

contract SearchCorrectness {
    -- What narrowing may and may not change about a query.

    @invariant RankedLimitKeepsTheFirstRows
        -- The scan hands on every row in the prefix the SQL sort needs, including OFFSET and LIMIT.
        -- One query cutoff accounts for all unopened, queued, claimed and pending-verification work across the pinned partitions.
        -- Tree postings and older search files can contribute candidates to the same ranked point scan.
        -- Work with unknown rank has an unbounded ceiling until the query has stronger evidence.
        -- A worker replaces its claimed bound with its child bounds and verified rows in one publication.
        -- Every cutoff row is a resolved version whose primary payload satisfies the scan's predicates and supplies its score.
        -- A stale revision cannot suppress a different verified version of the same entity.
        -- A score tie remains eligible until the available SQL rank keys prove its order.
        -- Integer and certified canonical UUID bounds can order score ties across partitions.
        -- A UUID posting range's lower identity bound comes from its first document where publication certifies nondecreasing canonical IID order.
        -- The final projection gathers the retained rows at the pinned cut.

    @invariant ResultIndependentOfSearchFiles
        -- A query's rows are the same whichever search files exist, are readable, or are missing, including none.
        -- SEARCH_SCORE's value is a function of its arguments (ScoresFromArguments).

    @invariant CandidatesAreASuperset
        -- Every IID with a visible row satisfying MATCH at the query's valid and system time is in the candidate set.

    @invariant VisibilityIsResolved
        -- Whether a row is visible is decided by the scan's resolution over the candidate's full history.
        -- A document's rectangles only ever rule a candidate out: an event in another trie or the live index can cut a run's rectangles and never widen them.
        -- A point read follows the pinned directory and adds accepted events from its durable and live suffix.
        -- Recorded typed entity keys delimit separate histories behind the same routing IID.
        -- A post-erase snapshot applies its erase frontier at earlier system times as well.
        -- An accepted event's origin identifies a relocated payload in primary Arrow.
        -- A packed point query can batch complete IID histories from the held packs and live segments into that same resolver.
        -- If a relevant source lacks complete version evidence, the primary history resolves those candidates.

    @invariant LiveSearchSharesTheCommitCut
        -- A committed native snapshot holds its admitted search postings alongside the primary live rows of that cut.
        -- A live posting page can retain its token list as a slice of the accepted delta under the delta buffers' reservation.
        -- A newer commit or block boundary does not advance search evidence held by an earlier snapshot.
        -- A partially admitted segment keeps its coverage frontier.
        -- Commit deltas and live postings read a selected text child once per Arrow batch and retain the existing input and page admission limits.
        -- If a query cannot cover the frontier within the accelerated tail budget, ordinary SQL evaluation supplies the answer.

    @invariant SearchFilesFromTheSnapshot
        -- A scan reads the search files its snapshot's catalog records, and those its node builds from the snapshot's data files, and no others.
        -- A node keeps recorded files by object key, and the files it builds by data-file key and columns, each naming immutable bytes, and uses one only while the database's storage is the one it was read through, so a kept file is never stale.
        -- A recorded Arrow file in the node's local store or disk cache is mapped from disk and its pages can be loaded on demand.
        -- A reader backed by the original bytes in memory holds its buffer-pool lease, so when the pool refuses a read for want of room, the node drops the files it keeps that way and reads again.
        -- A recorded Tantivy bundle can serve the checked Arrow batches from its conversion when the budget admits their retention.
        -- The retained batches and their Arrow slices keep their allocation charges.
        -- When retention is declined, the conversion writes the batches to a temporary Arrow file and serves them from its mapping.
        -- An Arrow map converted from a Tantivy bundle under a prepaid query budget retains its allocation charge until the map is released.
}

contract SealedDerivedSearchSegments {
    -- Search runs, term directories and vector columns built from a relation whose rows carry a protection label are sealed derived segments.
    -- A derived segment can be partially inverted back to its source text, so it carries its source's protection and is erased with it.
    -- Bijection writes the protected text of a labelled partition into its own derived search relation (`<partition>_search`), in the same commit as the sealed source row, and erases its rows with the source.

    @invariant OneLabelSetPerSegment
        -- A caller that labels rows places each label set in its own relation, and a search path is declared per relation.
        -- A run, a term directory and a vector column cover the events of one relation only, so each derived segment holds entries of exactly one label set.
        -- A ranked read names the relations its caller admitted and ranks only their segments, so the label filter applies before ranking.

    @invariant SealedUnderItsOwnSlot
        -- A labelled relation is searchable only on storage enrolled in sealing.
        -- Each derived segment is a durable unit of its own (R6), published under a fresh key slot of the relation's custody domain and never under the slot of the trie it was built from.
        -- A segment's plaintext exists only in memory under the reader's grant lease.
        -- Log frames and live segments that carry the source text keep the sealing and erasure of the primary events they hold.

    @invariant InventoryNamesTheSources
        -- A run's source receipt is its dependency inventory: lineage, replica interval, table, shard, block extents, row count and erase frontier.
        -- The inventory determines which runs an erasure affects without opening any segment.

    @invariant ErasureRederivesFromSurvivors
        -- An ERASE in the relation selects every run whose inventory covers an erased event.
        -- The lane re-derives each selected run from the surviving events under a fresh slot and publishes it by ordered run replacement.
        -- Once the replacement is published and the readers holding the old run have drained, the exact retirement owner withdraws the old run's slot under its deletion grant (R7).
        -- Pre-erasure copies of the old run in backups and object versions are then unreadable.
        -- From the erase onwards a ranked read is exact over the survivors: the erase frontier cuts the old run's rectangles until its replacement is published (VisibilityIsResolved).

    @invariant RebuildOnlyStructuresReportBuilding
        -- A term directory and an approximate vector generation cannot retract one entity's contribution.
        -- After an erase that affects them they serve nothing until rebuilt from the replacement runs, and a reader that needs them receives a building outcome, never an earlier generation.
        -- Exact ranking never needs them: its scores come from the held corpus or from explicit statistics.
        -- A term directory records the digest of the exact runs it covers; a reader whose active runs differ reports it for rebuild and ranks without its bounds.

    @invariant StatisticsFromTheAdmittedCorpus
        -- Corpus statistics come from the relations the reader admitted at its held cut, or from the five-argument SEARCH_SCORE with statistics the caller aggregated over its admitted rows.
        -- No ambient artefact, term directory or unadmitted relation contributes a count, so a score never discloses rows of a label set the reader did not admit.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface FullTextSearch {
    facing client: db/Client

    context db: db/Database

    provides:
        SearchIndexDefined(definition)
        SearchIndexDropped(name)
        ScanWithMatch(scan, predicate)

    contracts:
        demands SearchCorrectness
        demands SealedDerivedSearchSegments

    @guarantee MatchAtAnyTime
        -- MATCH answers at any valid time and system time the scan accepts, the default as-of-now included.

    @guarantee ScoresFromArguments
        -- The same document, query text and optional statistics produce the same score across scan plans, joins, time coordinates and artefact states.
        -- A caller may derive statistics by aggregating the rows of an explicitly selected corpus.
}

-- Black box helpers used in rule expressions:
--   search_entries(job)                — the file's record on its trie or on every member of its group
--   search_candidates(scan, predicate)  — steps 1–4 of the query plan
--   BuildAndUploadSearchFile(job)       — tokenise the data file's rows and upload the bundled index under the owner's artefact key
--   EachCandidateCutByItsEntitysLaterEvents(scan) — CutByLaterEvents' subtraction, before narrowing and ranking
--   EachResolvedRowMatchedFromItsSearchFileOrValue(scan, predicate) — MatchEvaluatedByTheScan's evaluation, in the scan, before its filter
--   retired_records(table)               — the records RetireSearchFiles retires (maintenance/src/artefact.rs: retired_artefacts)
--   RetiredSearchFilesDeleted(table)     — delete the retired records' objects, and list the table's artefact objects to delete the unrecorded ones RetireSearchFiles names (stray_artefacts)

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "How far does fan-out scale? A completed partition group can share one search file across four sibling tries. At what table size would files spanning several partition groups be useful?"
